{"title":"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Establishing_Parallelism_via_Homothety\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Establishing Parallelism via Homothety</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Definition_of_Point_and_Direction_of_Radii_()\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Definition of Point  and Direction of Radii (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/1/1f10294ae5858c1d3486b65e061c6b232959b5ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}D \\parallel G_{2}Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Perpendicularity_and_Tangency_at_Point\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Perpendicularity and Tangency at Point</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Congruence_and_Conclusion\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Congruence and Conclusion</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> are contained inside the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and are tangent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> at the distinct points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> passes through the center of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />.  The line passing through the two points of intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> meets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.  The lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/f/62f7eb82948ac9d7e8659abb37508b1015d8a3cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MA$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/6/856c130e0bb237c3edeb7e3c0246d40cc607e861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MB$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> meet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.\n</p><p>Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is tangent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Proof: Line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is Tangent to Circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> (IMO 1999 Problem 5)</b>\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:IMO1999_Problem5.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/42/IMO1999_Problem5.jpg/750px-IMO1999_Problem5.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"750\" height=\"422\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/42/IMO1999_Problem5.jpg/1125px-IMO1999_Problem5.jpg 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/4/42/IMO1999_Problem5.jpg 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Establishing_Parallelism_via_Homothety\">Establishing Parallelism via Homothety</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Establishing Parallelism via Homothety\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> be internally tangent to the large circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> at the distinct points of tangency <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Let the line passing through the two intersection points of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> meet circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, forming the common chord <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>First, we establish the parallelism involving circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> at the tangency point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p>\n<ul><li>By the Tangent-Chord Theorem, if we consider a common tangent line to both circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, the angles subtended by chords on the same line are equal to the respective inscribed angles.</li>\n<li>This implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/d/fbd49866ccbb94527a18244f3afa85fdcbe87eaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle MDC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/4/06459720ea0e1b5455af80004abbab14fb99f246.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle MBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> share a common vertex angle at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and have equal base angles (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/1/6213fd486c056c2ad50c781601e49e5718ba8b7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle MDC = \\angle MBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"149\" height=\"14\" />). Therefore, they are similar triangles:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/b/07b5215893e836a4d7f2b44996b69ba7505c0601.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\triangle MDC \\sim \\triangle MBA\\]\" width=\"153\" height=\"13\" />\n</p>\n<ul><li>Under a homothety (dilation) centered at the point of tangency <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/d/fbd49866ccbb94527a18244f3afa85fdcbe87eaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle MDC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> maps directly onto <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/4/06459720ea0e1b5455af80004abbab14fb99f246.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle MBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />. Since a homothety always maps a line to a parallel line, the base lines must be parallel:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/c/6ac892d44b548fe20c724c67e9969b7bee875e28.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[CD \\parallel AB\\]\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Next, we justify the relation involving the second circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and the constructed line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b6e51503abd50280c5d6703c03da6cfd2b0691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. By considering the homothety centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, which is the point of tangency between the large circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Under this homothety centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, any chord of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> maps onto a parallel chord of the large circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b6e51503abd50280c5d6703c03da6cfd2b0691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> be a chord of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> chosen such that its homothetic image from center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the chord <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> of the large circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li>Since a homothety always transforms a line into a parallel line, the triangle configurations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/8/ef8d0a5c651748d46abbf6fa04506f62bba01ecb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle EZN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f39d3f6da95fa66928b7a68e766f47a00425b45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" /> are homothetic with respect to the center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, directly guaranteeing that:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/5/35585560823066e944bd37bd23d925f854204081.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AB \\parallel EZ\\]\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Combining these results, we establish a robust system of three parallel lines across the entire configuration:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/a/41ab836ec43382357baaa42b0e58a6ae9cafd3ef.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[CD \\parallel AB \\parallel EZ\\]\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Furthermore, by the standard properties of intersecting circles, the line of centers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/a/8fa509f96d18be767c6663c4c087d29176bec847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" /> is strictly perpendicular to their common intersection line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Since our baseline system is completely parallel (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/19767ee371179801916a2c0fcfb0545e7d991f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD \\parallel AB \\parallel EZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"20\" />), the line of centers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/a/8fa509f96d18be767c6663c4c087d29176bec847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" /> must also be perpendicular to the chords <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b6e51503abd50280c5d6703c03da6cfd2b0691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> be the intersection point where the line of centers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/a/8fa509f96d18be767c6663c4c087d29176bec847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" /> crosses the chord <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />. This directly establishes the perpendicularity at vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/f/26fb935aa178b6d9f026ae23035c3819d1532eb7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle CKG_{2} = 90^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Definition_of_Point_and_Direction_of_Radii_.28.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition_of_Point_and_Direction_of_Radii_()\">Definition of Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> and Direction of Radii (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/1/1f10294ae5858c1d3486b65e061c6b232959b5ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}D \\parallel G_{2}Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Definition of Point  and Direction of Radii ()\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"9\" height=\"14\" /> be constructed such that it is tangent to circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. We extend this tangent line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"9\" height=\"14\" /> until it interacts with circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />, and we define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> as the point of contact on the circumference of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p>\n<ul><li>By the definition of a tangent line to a circle, the radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ecf443e5f2448e9458c97aa1aa513d28861f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"17\" /> is strictly perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"9\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/c/7ace9d46986e4108f1ef873b4a8c334753407b12.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle G_{1}DZ = 90^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"111\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>From the angle chasing of the variable parameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> within the established parallel system:\n</p>\n<ul><li>The radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ecf443e5f2448e9458c97aa1aa513d28861f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"17\" /> makes an angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> with respect to line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li>The radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/c/edc815fc20e4bdba9b9ef7095d256dedb3590e43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> makes an angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> with respect to line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b6e51503abd50280c5d6703c03da6cfd2b0691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li>Since the baseline system is parallel (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/d/41dec7ff924f4d09f5c78567bbd64b378a60897f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD \\parallel EZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" />) and both radii open at the exact same angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> relative to these baselines, the directional vectors of the two radii must be parallel:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/0/b10f33e7c97da7bc2c87676c74dc3c841fab71a8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[G_{1}D \\parallel G_{2}Z\\]\" width=\"91\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Perpendicularity_and_Tangency_at_Point\">Perpendicularity and Tangency at Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Perpendicularity and Tangency at Point\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since we have established that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/1/1f10294ae5858c1d3486b65e061c6b232959b5ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}D \\parallel G_{2}Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />, the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/6/fb6ccece197a8b1ba21442ca2f67312f2ac7a877.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> (line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"9\" height=\"14\" />) acts as a transversal line cutting across these two parallel radii. \n</p>\n<ul><li>By the consecutive interior angles theorem, the interior angles on the same side of a transversal must sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f0cd95f95238159d02dbcea0aca632c2928991c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />.</li>\n<li>This allows us to calculate and prove the exact angle at vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/3/77386e99bd77ff5b72fe55dd2390161a7e12f57f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle DZG_{2} = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle G_{1}DZ = 180^{\\circ} - 90^{\\circ} = 90^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"364\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c637f2f2bd75235e7d6897ac8541ddeadfa7a25c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DZG_{2} = 90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> lies on the circumference of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/6/a26ecd1398219af39f2da5e7f28e80961333e624.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}Z = r_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"17\" />), the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/6/fb6ccece197a8b1ba21442ca2f67312f2ac7a877.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is now rigorously proven to be tangent to circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p>Consequently, since line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/6/fb6ccece197a8b1ba21442ca2f67312f2ac7a877.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is simultaneously perpendicular to radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ecf443e5f2448e9458c97aa1aa513d28861f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"17\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/c/edc815fc20e4bdba9b9ef7095d256dedb3590e43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />, it acts as a common external tangent line to both circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec92aae78e752584119cd37a7dca7e812ba65efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Computing the remaining interior angles via the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> parameter yields:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/b/98b33aac823ca0850cf2cefe32a90c1bb4668338.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle G_{2}DZ = 45^{\\circ} - \\lambda/2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle DG_{2}Z = 45^{\\circ} + \\lambda/2\\]\" width=\"388\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Congruence_and_Conclusion\">Congruence and Conclusion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Congruence and Conclusion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Now, consider triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/4/5f41561ec629f4321b0d43282ba36cc59b139d91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DKG_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/0/7b0a7d62c2109a22cbd30ce23e97ac28136f3794.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DZG_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" />:\n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Shared Side:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/c/f5cdd15f76f3df4d1272bb6208f54ad28b1e9701.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DG_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"17\" /> is a common side to both triangles.</li>\n<li><b>Equal Angles:</b> From our angle chasing, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c5bda347f4337b0e84dabce961e6924710df82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DG_{2}K = \\angle DG_{2}Z = 45^{\\circ} + \\lambda/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"258\" height=\"20\" />.</li>\n<li><b>Equal Radii:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/0/7f0357088ad80981c46f6520198cf66d8661f912.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}K = G_{2}Z = r_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"17\" /> (both are radii of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />).</li></ol>\n<p>By the Side-Angle-Side (SAS) congruence criterion, we establish that:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/3/9339a2b5b3a1248f5660ea00f8d66594f46680e5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\triangle DKG_{2} \\cong \\triangle DZG_{2}\\]\" width=\"159\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Because the two triangles are congruent, their corresponding parts are equal (CPCTC). Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c637f2f2bd75235e7d6897ac8541ddeadfa7a25c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DZG_{2} = 90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"18\" />, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/0/2602053b07ce89c0802c80511265f8791d3d5350.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DKG_{2} = 90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" />, which perfectly matches our geometric setup where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/1/b0188265c89be7ae68647a807aafc5481e95dc2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{1}G_{2} \\perp CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"17\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />. This guarantees that the perpendicular distance from the center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> to the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is precisely equal to the radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/1/0f1886cc920b16bbd671a6e467021188fd057eca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is unconditionally tangent to circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35efd3745719d0ffc643a66eda552b59a4d60ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> independent of the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO\" title=\"1999 IMO\">1999 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1999 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017005300\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.049 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.067 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 737/1000000\nPost‐expand include 